      "text": "Since an integer when divided by 15 gives a remainder of 7, this integer can be expressed as \\(15k + 7\\) for some integer \\(k\\).\n\nTo find the remainder when this integer is divided by 3, we substitute \\(15k + 7\\) into the expression and simplify:\n\\[\n15k + 7 \\equiv 7 \\pmod{3}\n\\]\nSince \\(15k\\) is a multiple of 3, it contributes nothing to the remainder when divided by 3. Therefore, we only need to consider the remainder of 7 when divided by 3:\n\\[\n7 \\div 3 = 2 \\text{ remainder } 1 \\implies 7 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{3}\n\\]\nSo, the remainder when the integer is divided by 3 is 1.\n\nNext, to find the remainder when the same integer is divided by 5, we substitute \\(15k + 7\\) into the expression and simplify:\n\\[\n15k + 7 \\equiv 7 \\pmod{5}\n\\]\nSince \\(15k\\) is a multiple of 5, it contributes nothing to the remainder when divided by 5. Therefore, we only need to consider the remainder of 7 when divided by 5:\n\\[\n7 \\div 5 = 1 \\text{ remainder } 2 \\implies 7 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{5}\n\\]\nSo, the remainder when the integer is divided by 5 is 2.\n\nThe sum of the remainders when the integer is divided by 3 and by 5 is:\n\\[\n1 + 2 = 3\n\\]\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{3}\n\\]",
